he extreme ratios are inconsistent with a constant geometric ratio. However, let's check the 3rd term ratio to the 2nd term:\n\n\\[ \\frac{15}{10} = 1.5 \\]\n\nAnd check the 6th term ratio to the 5th term:\n\n\\[ \\frac{20}{25} = 0.8 \\]\n\nThese ratios are inconsistent as well. Given this, we need to find where the geometric sequence property becomes consistent. The geometric sequence property must hold for several terms when the seventh term is removed. We will verify the first few common ratios starting from the 5th term.\n\nFor the seventh term removal, one better geometric ratio can become apparent after checking a consistent pair ratio. The 5th term is better suited for consistent geometric checking:\n\n\\[ \\frac{25}{5} = 5 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{10}{25} = 2 \\]\n\nThis shows the inconsistency but \"menu\" rearrangement will bypass this inconsistency. The verified next consistent division would check between least common units 5/check this progression more exacting it better fits the geometric ratio property solution.\n\nThe final consistent check notated answer within \\( \\boxed{} \\) is examining any derived ratio of checked geometric backing principle criteria correctly adjusted excluding inconsistencies least expected:\n\n\\[\nr = \\boxed{5}\n\\]",
